The heat absorbed by the water is calculated using $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Given $ Q = 8.4 \times 10^6 \, \text{J} $, $ m = 100 \, \text{kg} $, $ c = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, and final temperature $ T_f = 40^\circ\text{C} $, solve for initial temperature $ X $:  
$$
\Delta T = \frac{Q}{mc} = \frac{8.4 \times 10^6}{100 \times 4.2 \times 10^3} = 20^\circ\text{C}
$$
$$
X = T_f - \Delta T = 40 - 20 = 20^\circ\text{C}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{20^\circ\text{C}}

The pressure exerted by the car is force (weight) divided by total contact area. Total contact area is 4 tires × 0.02 m² = 0.08 m². Weight = mass × gravity (10 N/kg). Given pressure $ P = 1.5 \times 10^5 \, \text{Pa} $, we solve for mass $ X $:  
$$
X = \frac{P \times \text{area}}{\text{gravity}} = \frac{1.5 \times 10^5 \times 0.08}{10} = 1200 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{1200 kg}

To find the empty vehicle mass $ X $, we use the work done by the traction force. The resistance force is $ 0.04 \times $ total weight, where total weight is $ (X + 60) \times 10 \, \text{N/kg} $. The resistance force becomes $ 0.04 \times (X + 60) \times 10 = 0.4(X + 60) \, \text{N} $. Work done by traction is force times distance:  
$$
0.4(X + 60) \times 10,000 = 5.76 \times 10^6 \, \text{J}
$$
Solving:  
$$
X + 60 = \frac{5.76 \times 10^6}{4000} = 1440 \implies X = 1440 - 60 = 1380 \, \text{kg}
$$
Final answer: \boxed{1380 \text{kg}}

The bus travels at constant speed, so engine power equals resistance power. Energy from natural gas is $ E = m_{\text{gas}} \cdot q_{\text{natural gas}} \cdot \eta $, where $ m_{\text{gas}} = 0.004 \, \text{kg} $, $ q_{\text{natural gas}} = 4.0 \times 10^8 \, \text{J/kg} $, and $ \eta = 0.45 $. Calculating $ E = 0.004 \times 4.0 \times 10^8 \times 0.45 = 7.2 \times 10^5 \, \text{J} $. Work done against resistance is $ W = F_{\text{resistance}} \cdot X $, where $ F_{\text{resistance}} = 1800 \, \text{N} $. Setting $ W = E $:  
$$
1800 \cdot X = 7.2 \times 10^5 \implies X = \frac{7.2 \times 10^5}{1800} = 400 \, \text{m}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{400 \text{m}}

The heat absorbed by the aluminum block is calculated using $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Rearranging for mass $ m $:  
$$
m = \frac{Q}{c\Delta T} = \frac{1.8 \times 10^4 \, \text{J}}{(0.9 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)}) \times 40 \, \text{°C}} = \frac{1.8 \times 10^4}{3.6 \times 10^4} = 0.5 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{0.5 kg}

To determine [X], the mass of hot water heated daily, we use the energy balance. The saved LPG mass (0.3 kg) corresponds to the energy previously provided by LPG. The energy required to heat [X] kg of water is $ Q = X \cdot 4.2 \times 10^3 \cdot \Delta T $. Assuming $ \Delta T = 40^\circ \text{C} $ (heating from 20°C to 60°C), this equals the energy from LPG: $ Q = 0.7 \cdot 0.3 \cdot 8.0 \times 10^7 = 1.68 \times 10^7 $ J. Solving for $ X $:  
$$
X = \frac{1.68 \times 10^7}{4.2 \times 10^3 \cdot 40} = 100 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{100 kg}

The pressure exerted by the car is given by $ P = \frac{F}{A} $, where $ F = mg $ and $ A $ is the total contact area. Rearranging for mass:  
$$
m = \frac{P \cdot A}{g}
$$  
Given $ P = 2 \times 10^5 \, \text{Pa} $, $ A = 4 \times 0.02 \, \text{m}^2 = 0.08 \, \text{m}^2 $, and $ g = 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 $:  
$$
m = \frac{2 \times 10^5 \cdot 0.08}{9.8} \approx 1632.65 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Rounding to two significant figures, the total mass $ [X] $ should be $ 1.6 \times 10^3 \, \text{kg} $.  

Final answer: \boxed{1.6 \times 10^3 \text{kg}}

To find [X], the mass of water, use the given gas mass (0.01 kg) and efficiency. The energy from gas is $ 0.5 \times 0.01 \, \text{kg} \times 4.2 \times 10^7 \, \text{J/kg} = 2.1 \times 10^5 \, \text{J} $. This equals the heat required: $ Q = m_{\text{water}} \times 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·℃)} \times 50 \, \text{℃} $. Solving for $ m_{\text{water}} $:  
$$
m_{\text{water}} = \frac{2.1 \times 10^5}{4.2 \times 10^3 \times 50} = 1 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{1 kg}

The heat from burning gas is $ Q = qV = 4.0 \times 10^7 \, \text{J/m}^3 \times 0.21 \, \text{m}^3 = 8.4 \times 10^6 \, \text{J} $. This equals the heat absorbed by water: $ Q = mc\Delta T $, where $ m = 100 \, \text{kg} $, $ c = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·℃)} $, and $ \Delta T = X - 20 $. Solving for $ X $:  
$$
X - 20 = \frac{8.4 \times 10^6}{100 \times 4.2 \times 10^3} = 20 \implies X = 40 \, \text{℃}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{40}

To determine the mass of water (X), use the heat absorbed formula $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Given $ Q = 6.3 \times 10^4 \, \text{J} $, $ c_{\text{water}} = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, and $ \Delta T = 30 \, \text{°C} $, solve for $ m $:  
$$
m = \frac{Q}{c \Delta T} = \frac{6.3 \times 10^4}{4.2 \times 10^3 \times 30} = \frac{6.3 \times 10^4}{1.26 \times 10^5} = 0.5 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{0.5 kg}

The heat absorbed by the water is calculated using $ Q = m_{\text{water}} c_{\text{water}} \Delta T $. Given $ Q = 2.016 \times 10^3 \, \text{J} $, $ c_{\text{water}} = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, and $ \Delta T = 18^\circ \text{C} - 12^\circ \text{C} = 6^\circ \text{C} $, solve for $ m_{\text{water}} $:  
$$
m_{\text{water}} = \frac{2.016 \times 10^3}{4.2 \times 10^3 \times 6} = \frac{2016}{25200} = 0.08 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{0.08 kg}

The work done by traction is force times distance: $ W = F \cdot X $. Given $ W = 1.6 \times 10^7 \, \text{J} $ and $ F = 1000 \, \text{N} $, solving for $ X $:  
$$
X = \frac{W}{F} = \frac{1.6 \times 10^7}{1000} = 1.6 \times 10^4 \, \text{m}
$$  
The battery energy and combustion efficiency are irrelevant to calculating $ X $ directly from the given traction work.  
Final answer: \boxed{1.6 \times 10^4 \text{m}}

The work done by the bus in 300 seconds is $ W = P \cdot t = 140,000 \, \text{J/s} \times 300 \, \text{s} = 4.2 \times 10^7 \, \text{J} $. This equals the heat released by hydrogen: $ Q = m \cdot q_{\text{hydrogen}} $. Solving for mass $ m $:  
$$
m = \frac{Q}{q_{\text{hydrogen}}} = \frac{4.2 \times 10^7}{1.4 \times 10^8} = 0.3 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{0.3 kg}

