$$
X = \frac{P}{v} = \frac{2 \times 10^4 \ \text{W}}{20 \ \text{m/s}} = 1000 \ \text{N}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{1000 \text{N}}

To find the temperature $ X $, calculate the heat absorbed by water using the efficiency of the gas stove. The energy from gas is $ 0.013 \, \text{m}^3 \times 4.9 \times 10^7 \, \text{J/m}^3 = 6.37 \times 10^5 \, \text{J} $. Efficiency $ \eta = 64.3\% $ gives useful energy $ Q_{\text{water}} = 0.643 \times 6.37 \times 10^5 = 4.10 \times 10^5 \, \text{J} $. 

The heat required for water is $ Q = mc\Delta T $, where $ m = 1.5 \, \text{kg} $, $ c = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $. Solve $ 4.10 \times 10^5 = 1.5 \times 4.2 \times 10^3 \times (X - 20) $, yielding $ X - 20 = 65.01 $, so $ X = 85.01^\circ \text{C} $. 

Final answer: \boxed{85.0^\circ \text{C}}

The heat released by the hot water equals the heat absorbed by the milk. Using $ Q = mc\Delta T $, the water's heat release is $ Q_{\text{release}} = 1 \, \text{kg} \times 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·℃)} \times X $. Given $ Q_{\text{release}} = 2.1 \times 10^4 \, \text{J} $, solving for $ X $:  
$$
X = \frac{2.1 \times 10^4}{4.2 \times 10^3} = 5 \, \text{℃}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{5℃}

The heat released by water is calculated using $ q = mc\Delta T $. Given $ q = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J} $, $ m = 0.1 \, \text{kg} $, $ c = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, and final temperature $ T_f = 10^\circ\text{C} $, solving for initial temperature $ T_i $:

$$
\Delta T = T_f - T_i = -\frac{q}{mc} = -\frac{4.2 \times 10^3}{0.1 \times 4.2 \times 10^3} = -10^\circ\text{C}
$$

Thus, $ T_i = T_f + 10^\circ\text{C} = 20^\circ\text{C} $. The duration of the process is irrelevant here. Final answer: \boxed{20°C}

The electric kettle's energy input is $ P \cdot t = 800 \, \text{W} \cdot 500 \, \text{s} = 400{,}000 \, \text{J} $. The energy required to heat the water is $ Q = X \cdot c \cdot \Delta T = X \cdot 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} \cdot 60 \, \text{°C} $. Efficiency is $ \eta = \frac{Q}{\text{Energy input}} \cdot 100\% $. Given $ \eta = 94.5\% $, solving for $ X $:  
$$
X = \frac{0.945 \cdot 400{,}000}{4.2 \times 10^3 \cdot 60} = \frac{378{,}000}{252{,}000} = 1.5 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{1.5 kg}

The work done by the traction force equals the resistance force times distance. Resistance is 0.1×weight = 0.1×m×g. Distance is 100 km = 1×10^5 m. Work $ W = 0.1 \times m \times g \times d $. Solving for $ m $:  
$$
m = \frac{W}{0.1 \times g \times d} = \frac{9 \times 10^7}{0.1 \times 10 \times 10^5} = \frac{9 \times 10^7}{1 \times 10^5} = 900 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{900 kg}

The problem states the time required to heat water is 420 s with an 80% efficient kettle. Using the formula $ t = \frac{m c \Delta T}{P \eta} $, where $ m = \rho V $, and solving for volume $ V $:  
$$
420 = \frac{(\rho V) c \Delta T}{P \eta} \implies V = \frac{t P \eta}{\rho c \Delta T}
$$  
Assuming $ \Delta T = 80^\circ \text{C} $, $ \rho = 1.0 \times 10^3 \, \text{kg/m}^3 $, $ c = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, $ P = 1000 \, \text{W} $, $ \eta = 0.8 $:  
$$
V = \frac{420 \times 1000 \times 0.8}{1.0 \times 10^3 \times 4.2 \times 10^3 \times 80} = \frac{336000}{336000} = 1.0 \, \text{L}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{1.0 \text{ L}}

To determine the value of [X], which represents the height at which the aircraft hovers before dropping water, we use the power formula $ P = \frac{mgh}{t} $, where $ m = 200 \, \text{kg} $, $ g = 10 \, \text{N/kg} $, $ t = 20 \, \text{s} $, and $ P = 1.5 \times 10^4 \, \text{W} $. Solving for $ h $:

$$
h = \frac{P \cdot t}{m \cdot g} = \frac{(1.5 \times 10^4) \cdot 20}{200 \cdot 10} = \frac{3 \times 10^5}{2 \times 10^3} = 150 \, \text{m}
$$

Final answer: \boxed{150 m}

The work done by the traction force equals the resistance force multiplied by distance. Since the truck moves at constant speed, traction force equals resistance force (0.02X). Work $ W = F \cdot d $, so $ 2.4 \times 10^8 = 0.02X \cdot 4 \times 10^4 $. Solving for $ X $:  
$$
X = \frac{2.4 \times 10^8}{0.02 \times 4 \times 10^4} = \frac{2.4 \times 10^8}{8 \times 10^2} = 3 \times 10^5 \text{N}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{3\times 10^5 \text{N}}

The thermal efficiency η is given by η = W/Q, where W = F·d and Q = m·q. With η = 35% = 0.35, distance d = 5600 m, fuel mass m = 1.2 kg, and calorific value q = 4×10⁷ J/kg, calculate resistance [X].  
$$
0.35 = \frac{X \cdot 5600}{1.2 \cdot 4 \times 10^7} \implies X = \frac{0.35 \cdot 1.2 \cdot 4 \times 10^7}{5600} = \frac{1.68 \times 10^7}{5600} = 3 \times 10^3 \text{ N}
$$
Final answer: \boxed{3000 \text{ N}}

