The efficiency η of the boiler is given by η = (Q_water / Q_gas) × 100%. The heat absorbed by water is Q_water = m × c_water × ΔT = m × 4.2×10³ × 70. The energy from gas is Q_gas = 7 × 4.2×10⁷. Substituting η = 40% (0.4):  
$$
0.4 = \frac{m \times 4.2 \times 10^3 \times 70}{7 \times 4.2 \times 10^7}
$$  
Simplify:  
$$
0.4 = \frac{m \times 10^3 \times 70}{7 \times 10^7} \implies 0.4 = \frac{m \times 10^{-3} \times 10}{1} \implies m = \frac{0.4}{10^{-3}} = 400 \text{ kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{400 \text{ kg}}

The solar energy received by the water heater over 5 hours is given as 2.1×10⁷ J. To find the hourly rate [X], divide the total energy by the time:  
$$
X = \frac{2.1 \times 10^7 \, \text{J}}{5 \, \text{h}} = 4.2 \times 10^6 \, \text{J/h}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{4.2 \times 10^6 \text{ J/h}}

The heat absorbed by the water is calculated using $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Given $ Q = 8.4 \times 10^6 \, \text{J} $, $ m = 50 \, \text{kg} $, $ c = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, and final temperature $ T_f = 60^\circ \text{C} $, solve for initial temperature $ T_i $:

$$
\Delta T = \frac{Q}{mc} = \frac{8.4 \times 10^6}{50 \times 4.2 \times 10^3} = 40^\circ \text{C}
$$

$$
T_i = T_f - \Delta T = 60^\circ \text{C} - 40^\circ \text{C} = 20^\circ \text{C}
$$

Final answer: \boxed{20^\circ \text{C}}

To find the total mass [X] of the bus, use the pressure formula $ P = \frac{F}{A} $, where $ F = mg $. Rearranging gives $ X = \frac{P \cdot A}{g} $. Substituting $ P = 5 \times 10^5 \, \text{Pa} $, $ A = 0.06 \, \text{m}^2 $, and $ g = 10 \, \text{N/kg} $:  
$$
X = \frac{5 \times 10^5 \cdot 0.06}{10} = \frac{3 \times 10^4}{10} = 3 \times 10^3 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{3000 kg}

